Fillet: the corner at a tangent ride's end (1722) where the other rounding
reaches past the ride's point, so that three blends meet on the ride's
line (mitcad#1926, after 1722).

A wall 2.5 wide whose top's edges are rounded with 2 and 1, an end's edge
at the rounding of 2 rounded with 1.2 or 1.5 (or at the rounding of 1 with
1.7): that rounding's contact on the end face meets the other blend of the
ride, past the ride's point. And GitHub #371's tower, its top rounded with
2 and 1 and its legs' inner front edges with 0.5 (the ride's point lies
0.285 from the inner side there). 1722's corner did not take it and the
corner failed (the blends intersecting).

- PerformTwoCornerAtRideEnd: where the small's contact on the face at the
  end meets the big's blend past the ride's line (or not at all), the
  corner of three blends: Q, where the ride's line meets the small's blend;
  the small cut by the big's blend from the crossing of their contacts on
  the common face to Q and by the ride's other blend (U) from Q to P*,
  where its contact on the face at the end meets U's blend; the big cut by
  the small's blend up to Q, its point on the ride's line there; U cut by
  the small's blend from Q to P* and by the face at the end from P* to its
  point on its arc there, so that its own corner at its vertex is made here
  (its end in the data structure). Each curve goes into the data structure
  on its surfaces and the last on the face at the end, its transition there
  FORWARD where the vertex of U's edge on that face, which goes, lies on
  the curve's right about the surface's own normal (outside every tangent
  of U's section between its contacts, for either kind of rounding).
- Compute: the corners of two stripes one of which is a tangent ride's are
  made before the others, so that such a corner finds U's end not made yet.

